1
01:00:00,033 --> 01:00:02,666
<b>In 1930, a Dutch scholar named Snouck</b>

2
01:00:02,666 --> 01:00:04,666
<b>Hurgronje examined the Arabic</b>

3
01:00:04,666 --> 01:00:06,333
<b>original of an American treaty.</b>

4
01:00:07,066 --> 01:00:08,633
<b>The next year, the United States</b>

5
01:00:08,633 --> 01:00:09,433
<b>government printing</b>

6
01:00:09,433 --> 01:00:11,433
<b>office published what he found,</b>

7
01:00:12,266 --> 01:00:14,099
<b>what he found the State Department</b>

8
01:00:14,099 --> 01:00:15,766
<b>couldn't explain. And,</b>

9
01:00:16,166 --> 01:00:18,033
<b>almost 100 years later, neither</b>

10
01:00:18,033 --> 01:00:20,833
<b>can anyone else. When America declared</b>

11
01:00:20,833 --> 01:00:23,599
<b>independence in 1776, it lost the</b>

12
01:00:23,599 --> 01:00:24,400
<b>protection of the British</b>

13
01:00:24,500 --> 01:00:27,266
<b>Navy. Almost immediately, the corsairs</b>

14
01:00:27,266 --> 01:00:28,400
<b>of North Africa</b>

15
01:00:28,400 --> 01:00:30,633
<b>pounced. American merchant ships</b>

16
01:00:30,633 --> 01:00:32,699
<b>were being seized, their crews were held</b>

17
01:00:32,699 --> 01:00:34,466
<b>for ransom. Without a</b>

18
01:00:34,466 --> 01:00:36,366
<b>big navy to its name, the</b>

19
01:00:36,366 --> 01:00:37,866
<b>New Republic's only defense was</b>

20
01:00:37,866 --> 01:00:42,166
<b>diplomacy. So in 1796, American agents</b>

21
01:00:42,166 --> 01:00:43,666
<b>negotiated a peace agreement.</b>

22
01:00:44,366 --> 01:00:47,366
<b>That document was the 1796 Treaty of</b>

23
01:00:47,366 --> 01:00:49,133
<b>Tripoli. It was signed at</b>

24
01:00:49,133 --> 01:00:50,866
<b>Tripoli on November 4th,</b>

25
01:00:51,266 --> 01:00:54,866
<b>then certified at Algiers in January 1797</b>

26
01:00:54,866 --> 01:00:56,833
<b>by Joel Barlow, the United</b>

27
01:00:56,833 --> 01:00:58,933
<b>States Consul. President Adams</b>

28
01:00:58,933 --> 01:01:00,400
<b>transmitted the document to the Senate.</b>

29
01:01:01,166 --> 01:01:03,066
<b>On Wednesday, June 7th,</b>

30
01:01:03,066 --> 01:01:05,733
<b>1797, the Senate voted on it.</b>

31
01:01:06,133 --> 01:01:07,766
<b>Adams signed the ratification three days</b>

32
01:01:07,766 --> 01:01:10,066
<b>later. The Arabic original</b>

33
01:01:10,066 --> 01:01:12,066
<b>of that same treaty was held</b>

34
01:01:12,233 --> 01:01:14,233
<b>for over a century in the State</b>

35
01:01:14,233 --> 01:01:16,566
<b>Department file. Here's the most famous</b>

36
01:01:16,566 --> 01:01:17,733
<b>article of that treaty,</b>

37
01:01:18,266 --> 01:01:20,866
<b>Article 11. As the government of the</b>

38
01:01:20,866 --> 01:01:22,066
<b>United States of America</b>

39
01:01:22,066 --> 01:01:23,466
<b>is not in any sense founded</b>

40
01:01:23,466 --> 01:01:25,766
<b>on the Christian religion, as it has in</b>

41
01:01:25,766 --> 01:01:27,133
<b>itself no character of</b>

42
01:01:27,133 --> 01:01:29,133
<b>enmity against the laws, religion,</b>

43
01:01:29,133 --> 01:01:31,566
<b>or tranquility of Musselmen, the Senate</b>

44
01:01:31,566 --> 01:01:33,466
<b>voted yes. The Senate</b>

45
01:01:33,466 --> 01:01:35,966
<b>Journal records it on page 244.</b>

46
01:01:37,333 --> 01:01:40,466
<b>But here's what Hurgronje found. In 1930,</b>

47
01:01:40,966 --> 01:01:41,699
<b>the United States</b>

48
01:01:41,699 --> 01:01:43,666
<b>Department hired a Dutch scholar,</b>

49
01:01:43,933 --> 01:01:46,233
<b>Dr. Snouck Hurgronje of the University of</b>

50
01:01:46,233 --> 01:01:48,566
<b>Leiden, to examine the Arabic original.</b>

51
01:01:49,166 --> 01:01:52,066
<b>He produced an annotated translation. The</b>

52
01:01:52,066 --> 01:01:53,066
<b>US government printing</b>

53
01:01:53,066 --> 01:01:54,266
<b>office published it the</b>

54
01:01:54,266 --> 01:01:57,166
<b>next year in volume two of a multi-volume</b>

55
01:01:57,166 --> 01:01:58,266
<b>work edited by the</b>

56
01:01:58,266 --> 01:01:59,366
<b>State Department historian</b>

57
01:01:59,599 --> 01:02:03,533
<b>Hunter Miller. And on page 371, Hurgronje</b>

58
01:02:03,533 --> 01:02:05,633
<b>wrote this. The 11th</b>

59
01:02:05,633 --> 01:02:07,066
<b>article of the Barlow translation</b>

60
01:02:07,533 --> 01:02:09,866
<b>has no equivalent whatever in the Arabic.</b>

61
01:02:10,633 --> 01:02:11,833
<b>The Arabic text opposite</b>

62
01:02:11,833 --> 01:02:13,633
<b>that article is a letter</b>

63
01:02:13,633 --> 01:02:16,066
<b>from Hassan Pasha of Algiers to Yussuf</b>

64
01:02:16,066 --> 01:02:18,300
<b>Pasha of Tripoli. The</b>

65
01:02:18,300 --> 01:02:20,266
<b>Arabic version of the treaty does</b>

66
01:02:20,266 --> 01:02:22,666
<b>not contain Article 11. It was not</b>

67
01:02:22,666 --> 01:02:24,866
<b>mistranslated or paraphrased.</b>

68
01:02:25,333 --> 01:02:27,066
<b>The Arabic texts simply didn't</b>

69
01:02:27,066 --> 01:02:29,833
<b>contain the article. This letter wasn't a</b>

70
01:02:29,833 --> 01:02:31,066
<b>secret Islamic manifesto.</b>

71
01:02:31,500 --> 01:02:32,766
<b>When Hurgronje translated the</b>

72
01:02:32,833 --> 01:02:35,366
<b>text, he found a letter drawn up by a</b>

73
01:02:35,366 --> 01:02:37,033
<b>stupid secretary who just</b>

74
01:02:37,033 --> 01:02:38,133
<b>knew a certain number of</b>

75
01:02:38,133 --> 01:02:40,866
<b>bombastic words but entirely failed to</b>

76
01:02:40,866 --> 01:02:42,533
<b>catch their real meaning. A</b>

77
01:02:42,533 --> 01:02:44,333
<b>piece of literal nonsense.</b>

78
01:02:45,400 --> 01:02:47,400
<b>So if the Arabic is a different document</b>

79
01:02:47,400 --> 01:02:49,033
<b>entirely, what exactly</b>

80
01:02:49,033 --> 01:02:50,133
<b>did the Senate ratify?</b>

81
01:02:51,266 --> 01:02:54,000
<b>They ratified the English text. As Hunter</b>

82
01:02:54,000 --> 01:02:56,033
<b>Miller noted in 1931, it</b>

83
01:02:56,033 --> 01:02:57,900
<b>has always been deemed a text</b>

84
01:02:57,933 --> 01:03:00,266
<b>of the treaty. The Christian religion</b>

85
01:03:00,266 --> 01:03:02,466
<b>language is real, it was</b>

86
01:03:02,466 --> 01:03:04,699
<b>ratified and it carries the force</b>

87
01:03:04,699 --> 01:03:07,566
<b>of law. No serious historian disputes</b>

88
01:03:07,566 --> 01:03:10,433
<b>that. So how did this clerical mistake</b>

89
01:03:10,433 --> 01:03:11,733
<b>get bound in place of</b>

90
01:03:11,733 --> 01:03:13,666
<b>one of the most debated articles in</b>

91
01:03:13,666 --> 01:03:14,433
<b>American diplomatic</b>

92
01:03:14,433 --> 01:03:18,199
<b>history? Very simple. Because the</b>

93
01:03:18,199 --> 01:03:20,300
<b>American who signed it couldn't read it.</b>

94
01:03:20,733 --> 01:03:22,633
<b>As historian Denise Spellberg notes,</b>

95
01:03:23,300 --> 01:03:25,033
<b>neither Joel Barlow nor his</b>

96
01:03:25,066 --> 01:03:26,866
<b>American colleagues could read Arabic.</b>

97
01:03:27,466 --> 01:03:28,533
<b>They relied on the Spanish</b>

98
01:03:28,533 --> 01:03:30,133
<b>consul to verify the seals.</b>

99
01:03:31,066 --> 01:03:33,133
<b>Barlow attested to the accuracy of the</b>

100
01:03:33,133 --> 01:03:34,833
<b>document, but in Spellberg's</b>

101
01:03:34,833 --> 01:03:36,400
<b>phrasing, he probably never</b>

102
01:03:36,400 --> 01:03:39,066
<b>knew that Article 11 never existed in an</b>

103
01:03:39,066 --> 01:03:40,833
<b>Arabic translation. It</b>

104
01:03:40,833 --> 01:03:42,966
<b>wasn't a forgery or a big hoax.</b>

105
01:03:43,566 --> 01:03:45,533
<b>It was a translation gap that nobody in</b>

106
01:03:45,533 --> 01:03:47,400
<b>1797 was equipped to</b>

107
01:03:47,400 --> 01:03:50,533
<b>catch. Which is why in 1797</b>

108
01:03:51,033 --> 01:03:53,966
<b>this article was not a big deal. Adams</b>

109
01:03:53,966 --> 01:03:54,666
<b>didn't say anything</b>

110
01:03:54,666 --> 01:03:57,033
<b>publicly. Jefferson, then vice</b>

111
01:03:57,033 --> 01:03:59,766
<b>president, didn't comment on it. The</b>

112
01:03:59,766 --> 01:04:00,733
<b>Senate Journal records</b>

113
01:04:00,733 --> 01:04:02,466
<b>no debate on Article 11.</b>

114
01:04:03,566 --> 01:04:05,300
<b>Historians point to two preserved</b>

115
01:04:05,300 --> 01:04:07,533
<b>reactions. The first came from William</b>

116
01:04:07,533 --> 01:04:08,933
<b>Cobbett, a Federalist</b>

117
01:04:08,933 --> 01:04:11,233
<b>editor in Philadelphia in the porcupine</b>

118
01:04:11,233 --> 01:04:14,566
<b>gazette on June 23rd 1797.</b>

119
01:04:15,033 --> 01:04:16,466
<b>Cobbett wrote that the article</b>

120
01:04:16,900 --> 01:04:18,666
<b>certainly looks a little like trampling</b>

121
01:04:18,666 --> 01:04:20,699
<b>upon the cross. The other</b>

122
01:04:20,699 --> 01:04:22,633
<b>was deeply personal. The</b>

123
01:04:22,633 --> 01:04:24,766
<b>diplomat who signed the treaty, Joel</b>

124
01:04:24,766 --> 01:04:26,466
<b>Barlow, had once been the</b>

125
01:04:26,466 --> 01:04:27,966
<b>star student of Timothy Dwight,</b>

126
01:04:28,466 --> 01:04:30,133
<b>the conservative president of Yale.</b>

127
01:04:31,099 --> 01:04:33,300
<b>Disgusted by his former protege's radical</b>

128
01:04:33,300 --> 01:04:35,066
<b>turn, Dwight publicly</b>

129
01:04:35,066 --> 01:04:37,766
<b>denounced him and ordered Barlow's</b>

130
01:04:37,766 --> 01:04:38,833
<b>portrait stripped from</b>

131
01:04:38,833 --> 01:04:39,766
<b>the walls of the university.</b>

132
01:04:40,633 --> 01:04:43,500
<b>Hurgronje read both languages. He noticed</b>

133
01:04:43,500 --> 01:04:45,833
<b>the gap. Hunter Miller on</b>

134
01:04:45,833 --> 01:04:48,699
<b>page 384 of his 1931 volume</b>

135
01:04:49,033 --> 01:04:51,933
<b>called it "most extraordinary and wholly</b>

136
01:04:51,933 --> 01:04:54,000
<b>unexplained." No</b>

137
01:04:54,000 --> 01:04:55,333
<b>scholar since has explained</b>

138
01:04:55,366 --> 01:04:57,466
<b>it. But the strangest part of this</b>

139
01:04:57,466 --> 01:05:00,433
<b>discrepancy isn't the missing text. It's</b>

140
01:05:00,433 --> 01:05:02,333
<b>what happened in the 133</b>

141
01:05:02,633 --> 01:05:06,233
<b>years before anyone noticed. Today this</b>

142
01:05:06,233 --> 01:05:07,833
<b>passage sits at the center of the</b>

143
01:05:07,833 --> 01:05:08,766
<b>argument about whether</b>

144
01:05:08,833 --> 01:05:10,466
<b>America was founded as a Christian</b>

145
01:05:10,466 --> 01:05:14,133
<b>nation. In those 133 years before</b>

146
01:05:14,133 --> 01:05:15,633
<b>Hurgronje or Miller even</b>

147
01:05:15,666 --> 01:05:17,800
<b>saw the file, something was already</b>

148
01:05:17,800 --> 01:05:19,666
<b>happening. Historian Jacob</b>

149
01:05:19,666 --> 01:05:21,966
<b>Crane argues that Americans did</b>

150
01:05:21,966 --> 01:05:24,366
<b>not immediately view Article 11 as</b>

151
01:05:24,366 --> 01:05:26,466
<b>definitive proof of a secular founding.</b>

152
01:05:26,933 --> 01:05:28,533
<b>Instead, that modern</b>

153
01:05:28,533 --> 01:05:31,033
<b>interpretation developed slowly over the</b>

154
01:05:31,033 --> 01:05:33,766
<b>19th and 20th centuries. As</b>

155
01:05:33,766 --> 01:05:35,099
<b>the country grappled with new</b>

156
01:05:35,133 --> 01:05:37,233
<b>domestic controversies that had nothing</b>

157
01:05:37,233 --> 01:05:38,733
<b>to do with Tripoli, such</b>

158
01:05:38,733 --> 01:05:40,500
<b>as whether to publicly fund</b>

159
01:05:40,500 --> 01:05:43,199
<b>Christian clergy or ban Sunday alcohol</b>

160
01:05:43,199 --> 01:05:45,266
<b>sales, activists began</b>

161
01:05:45,266 --> 01:05:46,900
<b>repurposing the treaty's language</b>

162
01:05:46,900 --> 01:05:49,733
<b>to support their own political arguments.</b>

163
01:05:50,533 --> 01:05:51,966
<b>Therefore, the secular</b>

164
01:05:51,966 --> 01:05:53,666
<b>reading of the text wasn't</b>

165
01:05:53,666 --> 01:05:57,266
<b>an original 1797 concept, but a later</b>

166
01:05:57,266 --> 01:05:58,699
<b>invention pieced together</b>

167
01:05:58,699 --> 01:06:00,166
<b>by generations fighting local</b>

168
01:06:00,233 --> 01:06:04,099
<b>culture wars. Crane writes on page 405,</b>

169
01:06:04,766 --> 01:06:05,933
<b>"the language of Article</b>

170
01:06:05,933 --> 01:06:08,066
<b>11 was wielded as often by</b>

171
01:06:08,066 --> 01:06:10,233
<b>Christian nationalists as by militant</b>

172
01:06:10,233 --> 01:06:14,300
<b>secularists." Today this English text is</b>

173
01:06:14,300 --> 01:06:15,300
<b>wielded as the ultimate</b>

174
01:06:15,300 --> 01:06:17,966
<b>weapon in a modern culture war. Article</b>

175
01:06:17,966 --> 01:06:19,766
<b>11 meant almost nothing</b>

176
01:06:19,766 --> 01:06:21,933
<b>until 19th century activists</b>

177
01:06:22,166 --> 01:06:24,266
<b>started arguing over the public funding</b>

178
01:06:24,266 --> 01:06:25,300
<b>of the Christian clergy.</b>

179
01:06:26,199 --> 01:06:28,066
<b>The text itself didn't change,</b>

180
01:06:28,099 --> 01:06:31,833
<b>but the culture did. People simply</b>

181
01:06:31,833 --> 01:06:34,433
<b>projected their new cultural divide onto</b>

182
01:06:34,433 --> 01:06:35,733
<b>an old piece of paper.</b>

